===Etymology===
{{back-formation|en|pseudonymous}}, from {{der|en|fr|pseudonyme|pos=a||pseudonymous}}, from {{der|en|grc|ψευδώνυμος}}, from {{m|grc|ψευδής|t=false}} and {{m|grc|ὄνυμα}}, a dialectal form of  {{m|grc|ὄνομα|t=name}}. {{surf|en|pseudo-|-onym}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈs(j)uː.də(ʊ).nɪm/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsu.də.nɪm/|/ˈsu.doʊ.nɪm/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-pseudonym.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-pseudonym.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[fictitious]] name (more [[literally]], a [[false]] [[name]]), as [[those]] [[use]]d by [[writer]]s and [[movie star]]s.
#: {{syn|en|anonym<id:pseudonym>}}
#: {{ant|en|autonym|orthonym|alethonym}}
#: {{hypo|en|allonym|;|ananym|;|stage name|;|pen name|nom de plume|;|nom de clavier|nom de Web|;|nom de guerre}}
#: {{cot|en|alias|;|nickname|moniker|handle}}
#: {{ux|en|The Reverend Charles Lutwidge Dodgson wrote "Alice's Adventures in Wonderland" under the '''pseudonym''' Lewis Carroll.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1911|author=w:H. G. Wells|title=s:The Obliterated Man|text=I doubt, indeed, whether I should not abandon the struggle altogether—leave this sad world of ordinary life for which I am so ill fitted, abandon the name of Cummins for some professional '''pseudonym''', complete my self-effacement, and—a thing of tricks and tatters, of posing and pretence—go upon the stage.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1928|author=w:H.P. Lovecraft|title=s:Supernatural Horror in Literature|text=The best example of its literary use so far are the German novel ''The Golem'', by Gustav Meyrink, and the drama ''The Dybbuk'', by the jewish writer using the '''pseudonym''' "Ansky".}}

